---
// G2 and G4, network classification (Ch. 9). G2: one rule on the talk's three networks (src/scripts/defence/trio.ts);
// under each network three histograms, empty until ▶, then computed live over the whole run so far, for the thesis's
// 100 timesteps (./print): the Shannon entropy and the Lempel–Ziv complexity of every node's row, as Miranda et al. 2016
// define them, and the neighbourhood density that Ch. 9 used; then a fingerprint in their place (a whorl, a loop, a
// tented arch). Every new run (a reset by hand, a step back over ▶, a switch of rule) draws a new random start. The title asks whether
// behaviour predicts topology, and under it the hunt: rules whose fingerprints are distinct. First the consensus-seeking rule: every network agrees, and the three fingerprints
// are one (a plain arch); then Miranda et al.'s rule from the same start, and three different prints. G4: what makes a
// good detective: the same two rules on their rings, a red arrow (the flipped neighbour of the sensitivity slide) at
// every change of answer between neighbouring slices, counted (the thesis's jaggedness, never named on the slide); our
// result, and the bridge to what consensus-seeking rules are good for instead.
import Slide from './Slide.astro';
import SimButton from './SimButton.astro';
import { phi } from './tex';
import { TRIO } from '../../scripts/defence/trio';
const rows = [
  { f: 'entropy', name: 'Shannon entropy' },
  { f: 'lz', name: 'Lempel–Ziv complexity' },
  { f: 'density', name: 'neighbourhood density' },
];
const rules = [
  { id: 'consensus', name: 'Consensus-seeking', r: 9, B: 488, S: 464 },
  { id: 'miranda', name: 'Miranda et al.', r: 9, B: 170, S: 340 },
];
/** How often a rule changes its answer between neighbouring slices, born and survive together (the thesis's J). */
const changes = ({ r, B, S }: { r: number; B: number; S: number }) => [B, S].reduce((n, m) => { for (let i = 1; i < r; i++) n += ((m >> i) ^ (m >> (i - 1))) & 1; return n; }, 0);
const sides = [
  { x: { r: 9, B: 488, S: 464 }, name: 'A consensus-seeking rule' },
  { x: { r: 9, B: 170, S: 340 }, name: 'Miranda et al.’s rule' },
];

// G2's fingerprints, one textbook pattern per network (src/scripts/defence/fingerprint.ts); all three become a plain
// arch once the networks look alike
const kinds = ['whorl', 'loop', 'tented'];
---
<Slide id="ch-classification" label="Does behaviour predict topology?" builds={5} class="g2" ch={9}>
  <h2>Does behaviour predict topology?</h2>
  <div class="g2-head">
    <p class="g2-lede">Hunting for distinct fingerprints</p>
    <div class="g2-rules" role="group" aria-label="Rule">
      {rules.map((r, i) => <button type="button" class="ctl" data-rule={r.id} aria-pressed={i ? 'false' : 'true'}>{r.name} <span set:html={phi(r.r, r.B, r.S)} /></button>)}
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="g2-grid">
    {rows.map((r, k) => <p class="g2-row" data-b="1" style={`grid-row: ${k + 3}`}>{r.name}</p>)}
    {TRIO.map((ty, c) => (
      <figure data-type={c} style={`grid-column: ${c + 2}`}>
        <figcaption>{ty.name}</figcaption>
        <div class="g2-net">
          <canvas class="net" aria-label={`the rule running on the ${ty.name} network`}></canvas>
          {c === 2 && <SimButton class="g2-btn" />}
        </div>
        {rows.map((r, k) => <canvas class="hist" style={`grid-row: ${k + 3}`} data-f={r.f} data-b="1" aria-label={`${r.name} of the ${ty.name} network, over the run so far`}></canvas>)}
        <div class="fp" data-b-only="3 5" aria-hidden="true">
          <canvas class="fp-own" data-kind={kinds[c]} data-seed={11 + c}></canvas>
          <canvas class="fp-same" data-kind="arch" data-seed="14"></canvas>
        </div>
      </figure>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p slot="say">The first application turns the question around: instead of asking what a rule does on a network, ask what it tells us about the network. Here one rule runs on the three networks of before, a random one, a small-world ring and a scale-free one, 150 nodes each, from a random start with three nodes in ten on: first the consensus-seeking rule of the previous slides. One click names what we will look at, three properties per network, still empty; the next starts the rule, for 100 timesteps, as in the thesis.</p>
  <p slot="say">To turn what the network does into numbers, look at every node over the whole run so far. How evenly does it split its time between on and off: its Shannon entropy. How many new pieces does it take to write its row of ons and offs down: its Lempel–Ziv complexity. And what share of its neighbours is on, timestep after timestep: the neighbourhood density, which Chapter 9 used. Each network gets three histograms, live on the slide. Under the consensus-seeking rule every network agrees on all off within about ten timesteps, and from then on every node does the same: the histograms keep piling up at the low end, on all three networks alike (on a longer run they would become exactly the same). On the page, the ↺ draws a new start; about one start in fifteen leaves the ring stuck instead.</p>
  <p slot="say">Together, the three histograms are the network’s fingerprint (drawn here as a real one), and a simple classifier can learn to tell the fingerprints apart. Under the consensus-seeking rule the three fingerprints are one and the same: a bad detective. The next click puts in the rule G. H. B. Miranda and colleagues used in 2016, when they started classifying networks this way, <Fragment set:html={phi(9, 170, 340)} /> on their own intervals (closed on the left, so not quite our symmetric ones), and runs it. It runs from a new random start, as every switch of rule does. Now the histograms differ: on the scale-free network its many nodes with only four links soon stay off for good, so its entropy has a second peak at 0, and on the ring nearly every node is on half of the time. And so do the fingerprints: a whorl, a loop and a tented arch, the textbook kinds. So we look for rules whose fingerprints are clearly different; the switch at the top does the same by hand. The thesis did this on 23 datasets, with histograms split by degree and a support-vector machine; this slide keeps the idea and shrinks the rest.</p>
</Slide>

<Slide label="What makes a good detective?" builds={3} class="g4" ch={9}>
  <h2>What makes a good detective?</h2>
  <div class="g4-two">
    {sides.map((sd, i) => (
      <figure class="jag" data-rule={i}>
        <p class="g4-name">{sd.name} <span set:html={phi(sd.x.r, sd.x.B, sd.x.S)} /></p>
        <canvas class="jrings" data-rule={`${sd.x.r},${sd.x.B},${sd.x.S}`} aria-label="the rule as two rings, with a red arrow at every change of answer between neighbouring slices"></canvas>
        <p class="g4-count" data-b="1"><b>{changes(sd.x)}</b> of {2 * (sd.x.r - 1)}</p>
      </figure>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="result" data-b="2"><b>Our result</b> On 13 of 23 datasets, the best detectives change their mind often, but not always. Searching there finds a rule within 5% of the best while trying only 10–20% of the rules.</p>
  <p class="bridge" data-b="3">Consensus-seeking rules make bad detectives. But they are good at something else: <b>finding who matters</b>.</p>
  <p slot="say">What makes the difference can be read off the rule itself. Back to the rings, and to the red arrow of the sensitivity slide: one neighbour more or fewer moves a node one slice along its ring. The next click draws that arrow wherever it changes the answer, between every two neighbouring slices where the rule says something else. The consensus-seeking rule changes its mind at 6 of the 16 steps, Miranda’s rule at 15: a rule that changes its mind often is a sensitive rule, since a small change in the neighbourhood often changes what a node does.</p>
  <p slot="say">This is our contribution to network classification, Chapter 9, work led by Lucas C. de Oliveira (with M. Rollier, J. M. Baetens and O. M. Bruno, under review). Over thousands of rules and 23 datasets, the best detectives change their mind often, but not always: at between half and nine in ten of the steps. That makes sense: such rules are sensitive enough to pick up how networks differ, but not so sensitive that it drowns in chaos; they behave in a complex way (a reading the thesis proposes but does not test). Miranda’s rule, at 15 of 16, sits just above that band: on these three networks it does well, but over all the datasets the best rules change their mind a little less often. On 13 of the 23 datasets, more often tends to classify better (correlations between about 0.2 and 0.46), and searching among the rules that change their mind often, but not always, finds a rule within 5% of the best while trying only 10 to 20% of the rules. On social and bioinformatics networks there is no such pattern, but the method itself is mediocre there as well. (The count is what the thesis calls the jaggedness: the neighbour part of the Boolean sensitivity with every slice weighed the same, whatever the network. The Boolean sensitivity itself shows the same pattern, slightly weaker.)</p>
  <p slot="say">So consensus-seeking rules make bad detectives. But they turn out to be good at something else: finding which nodes matter.</p>
</Slide>

<style>
  .g2-head { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: calc(40 * var(--u)); }
  .g2-rules { display: flex; gap: calc(12 * var(--u)); }
  .g2-rules .ctl { font-size: calc(28 * var(--u)); padding: calc(8 * var(--u)) calc(18 * var(--u)); }
  .g2-rules .ctl[aria-pressed="true"] { background: var(--ink); border-color: var(--ink); color: var(--sun); }
  .g2-lede { font-size: calc(40 * var(--u)); }
  .g2-grid {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: calc(250 * var(--u)) repeat(3, 1fr);
    grid-template-rows: calc(44 * var(--u)) calc(330 * var(--u)) repeat(3, calc(86 * var(--u))); gap: calc(10 * var(--u)) calc(40 * var(--u));
  }
  .g2-grid figure { margin: 0; grid-row: 1 / span 5; display: grid; grid-template-rows: subgrid; justify-items: center; position: relative; }
  .g2-grid figure > * { grid-column: 1; }
  .g2-grid figcaption { font-size: calc(34 * var(--u)); font-weight: 700; grid-row: 1; }
  .g2-net { position: relative; grid-row: 2; }
  .net { width: calc(330 * var(--u)); height: calc(330 * var(--u)); }
  .g2-net :global(.g2-btn) { right: calc(-70 * var(--u)); bottom: 0; }
  .hist { width: 100%; height: 100%; }
  .g2-row { grid-column: 1; display: flex; align-items: center; font-size: calc(30 * var(--u)); line-height: 1.15; font-weight: 700; }
  /* the fingerprint takes the place of the histograms, which fade far back */
  .fp { grid-row: 3 / span 3; width: 100%; height: 100%; position: relative; pointer-events: none; }
  .fp canvas { position: absolute; inset: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; transition: opacity 0.6s ease; }
  :global(.g2.alike) .fp-own, :global(.g2:not(.alike)) .fp-same { opacity: 0; }
  :global(.g2.printed) .hist { opacity: 0.12; }
  :global(.g2.printed) .g2-row { opacity: 0.35; }

  .g4-two { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: calc(80 * var(--u)); }
  .jag { margin: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; gap: calc(8 * var(--u)); }
  .g4-name { font-size: calc(38 * var(--u)); font-weight: 700; }
  .jrings { width: calc(820 * var(--u)); height: calc(330 * var(--u)); }
  .g4-count { font-size: calc(44 * var(--u)); color: var(--danger); }
  .g4-count b { font-size: 1.3em; }
  :global(.g4) .result { margin-top: auto; }
  .bridge { font-size: calc(44 * var(--u)); font-weight: 400; line-height: 1.25; }
</style>

<script>
  import { slide, sim, fresh, type How } from '../../scripts/defence/deck';
  import { stage, onSize, ticker, type Stage } from '../../scripts/defence/stage';
  import { picture, drawNet, type Picture } from '../../scripts/defence/netdraw';
  import { TRIO, trioNet, trioStart, RHO0 } from '../../scripts/defence/trio';
  import { fingerprinter, PRINT, type Feature, type Print } from '../../scripts/defence/print';
  import { type Rule, step, randomState } from '../../scripts/llna';
  import { ridges, drawPrint, type PrintKind } from '../../scripts/defence/fingerprint';
  import { drawRing, hop, type Ring } from '../../scripts/defence/donut';

  const RULES: Record<string, Rule> = { miranda: { r: 9, B: 170, S: 340, part: 'uni' }, consensus: { r: 9, B: 488, S: 464 } };
  const FEATS: Feature[] = ['entropy', 'lz', 'density'];

  /** One histogram as bars on a common height `top`; nothing yet while its window is empty. */
  function bars(st: Stage, h: number[] | null, top: number) {
    const { ctx, t } = st, base = st.h - 4, bw = st.w / (h?.length ?? 10);
    ctx.strokeStyle = t.muted; ctx.lineWidth = 2; ctx.beginPath(); ctx.moveTo(0, base); ctx.lineTo(st.w, base); ctx.stroke();
    if (!h) return;
    ctx.fillStyle = t.ink;
    h.forEach((v, b) => { const y = base - (Math.min(v, top) / top) * (st.h - 10); ctx.fillRect(b * bw + 2, y, bw - 4, base - y); });
  }

  // ── G2: the three networks, run by one rule; their fingerprints, live ──
  for (const s of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.slide.g2')) {
    const figs = Array.from(s.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('figure[data-type]')), buttons = Array.from(s.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-rule]'));
    let pick = 'consensus', pics: Picture[] | null = null, starts: Uint8Array[] | null = null, at = 0;
    // the networks are built when the slide is first drawn, not at page load
    const pic = () => (pics ??= TRIO.map((_, k) => picture(trioNet(k), 330, 10, k === 1 ? 3.2 : 3.8, k === 2 ? 0.6 : false)));
    type Col = { net: HTMLCanvasElement; hists: HTMLCanvasElement[]; a: Uint8Array; b: Uint8Array; fp: ReturnType<typeof fingerprinter> | null; print: Print };
    const cols: Col[] = figs.map((f) => ({ net: f.querySelector<HTMLCanvasElement>('canvas.net')!, hists: FEATS.map((x) => f.querySelector<HTMLCanvasElement>(`canvas.hist[data-f="${x}"]`)!), a: new Uint8Array(0), b: new Uint8Array(0), fp: null, print: { entropy: null, lz: null, density: null } }));
    // the starts: the networks' own when the slide is entered (the talk's run, checked by npm test), a new random one
    // for every run after that
    const fixed = () => { starts = pic().map((p, k) => trioStart(k, p.net.n)); };
    const another = () => { const rnd = fresh(); starts = pic().map((p) => randomState(p.net.n, RHO0, rnd)); };
    const reset = () => cols.forEach((c, k) => {
      const p = pic()[k];
      if (!starts) fixed();
      c.a = starts![k].slice(); c.b = new Uint8Array(p.net.n);
      c.fp ??= fingerprinter(p.net); c.fp.reset(); c.fp.push(c.a); c.print = c.fp.print();
    });
    function paint() {
      const ps = pic();
      cols.forEach((c, k) => { const st = stage(c.net); if (st) drawNet(st, ps[k], c.a); });
      // each row on one height, so the three columns compare
      FEATS.forEach((f, r) => {
        const top = Math.max(0.2, ...cols.map((c) => Math.max(0, ...(c.print[f] ?? [0]))));
        cols.forEach((c) => { const st = stage(c.hists[r]); if (st) bars(st, c.print[f], top); });
      });
    }
    const rule = () => RULES[pick];
    // twelve timesteps a second, for the thesis's 100: about eight seconds
    const round = () => cols.forEach((c, k) => { step(c.a, pic()[k].net, rule(), c.b); [c.a, c.b] = [c.b, c.a]; c.fp!.push(c.a); });
    const over = () => cols[0].fp!.t >= PRINT.T;
    const run = ticker(s, 12, () => { if (over()) return true; round(); cols.forEach((c) => (c.print = c.fp!.print())); return over(); }, paint);
    // the whole run at once, so a step back or a jump lands on the histograms the run ends with
    const settle = () => { while (!over()) round(); cols.forEach((c) => (c.print = c.fp!.print())); };
    const btn = s.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-simbtn]')!;
    // the prints follow the rule: one and the same under the consensus-seeking rule, one of each kind under Miranda's;
    // a switch runs the new rule from the beginning if the run had started
    const choose = (id: string) => {
      buttons.forEach((b) => b.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(b.dataset.rule === id)));
      s.classList.toggle('alike', id === 'consensus');
      if (id === pick) return;
      pick = id; reset(); paint();
      if (btn.dataset.run) run.play(true);
    };
    // a switch by hand, from a new start
    buttons.forEach((b) => b.addEventListener('click', () => { if (b.dataset.rule !== pick) another(); choose(b.dataset.rule!); }));
    // a reset by hand (↺, or a step back over ▶) draws a new start and empties the histograms; a jump keeps the start
    let rerolled = false;
    const going = sim(btn, 2, {
      reset() { run.play(false); reset(); if (!rerolled && at >= 3) settle(); rerolled = false; paint(); },
      play() { run.play(true); },
      reroll() { another(); rerolled = true; },
    });
    slide(s, {
      // the consensus-seeking rule up to build 3, where its prints take the histograms' place; build 4 takes them away
      // and runs Miranda et al.'s rule from a new start; build 5 shows its prints. A step back over build 4, or a jump,
      // lands on the other rule with its histograms formed.
      set(b: number, how: How) {
        at = b;
        s.classList.toggle('printed', b === 3 || b === 5);
        const want = b >= 4 ? 'miranda' : 'consensus';
        if (want === pick) return;
        // forward, the switch runs from a new start; a step back or a jump lands on the end of the networks' own run
        if (how === 'next') another(); else fixed();
        choose(want);
        if (how === 'next') { run.play(true); going(true); }
        else if (b >= 2) { settle(); paint(); if (how === 'prev') run.play(true); }
      },
      enter() { pick = ''; fixed(); choose('consensus'); },
      leave() { run.play(false); },
    });
    reset();
    for (const c of cols) { onSize(c.net, paint); c.hists.forEach((h) => onSize(h, paint)); }
    // the prints, as tall as the three histograms together
    for (const c of s.querySelectorAll<HTMLCanvasElement>('.fp canvas')) {
      const lines = ridges(c.dataset.kind as PrintKind, +c.dataset.seed!);
      onSize(c, () => { const st = stage(c); if (!st) return; const h = st.h - 4; drawPrint(st.ctx, lines, (st.w - h / 1.25) / 2, 2, h, st.t.ink); });
    }
  }

  // ── G4: the two rules of G2 on their rings; from the first click a red arrow at every change of answer between
  // neighbouring slices, the flipped neighbour of the sensitivity slide ──
  for (const s of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.slide.g4')) {
    const cs = Array.from(s.querySelectorAll<HTMLCanvasElement>('canvas.jrings'));
    let marked = false;
    const paint = (c: HTMLCanvasElement) => {
      const st = stage(c); if (!st) return;
      const [r, B, S] = c.dataset.rule!.split(',').map(Number), R = Math.min(st.w / 4 - 40, st.h / 2 - 34), w = R * 0.41;
      const rings: [Ring, number][] = [[{ x: st.w / 2 - R - 60, y: st.h / 2, R, w, on: false }, B], [{ x: st.w / 2 + R + 60, y: st.h / 2, R, w, on: true }, S]];
      for (const [g, m] of rings) {
        drawRing(st, g, r, m);
        if (marked) for (let i = 1; i < r; i++) if (((m >> i) ^ (m >> (i - 1))) & 1) hop(st, g, r, i - 0.36, i + 0.36, st.t.danger);
      }
    };
    slide(s, { set(b: number) { marked = b >= 1; cs.forEach(paint); } });
    cs.forEach((c) => onSize(c, () => paint(c)));
  }
</script>
